      "text": "To determine which number from the set \\(\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 15\\}\\) has the greatest number of divisors, we need to find the number of divisors for each of these integers.\n\nThe number of divisors of a positive integer \\(n\\) with prime factorization \\(n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \\cdots p_k^{e_k}\\) is given by \\((e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\\).\n\nLet's calculate the number of divisors for each integer from 1 to 15.\n\n1. \\(1 = p_1^0\\) has divisors: \\(1\\). (1 divisor)\n2. \\(2 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2\\). (2 divisors)\n3. \\(3 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 3\\). (2 divisors)\n4. \\(4 = p_1^2\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 4\\). (3 divisors)\n5. \\(5 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 5\\). (2 divisors)\n6. \\(6 = p_1^1 p_2^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 3, 6\\). (4 divisors)\n7. \\(7 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 7\\). (2 divisors)\n8. \\(8 = p_1^3\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 4, 8\\). (4 divisors)\n9. \\(9 = p_1^2\\) has divisors: \\(1, 3, 9\\). (3 divisors)\n10. \\(10 = p_1^1 p_2^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 5, 10\\). (4 divisors)\n11. \\(11 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 11\\). (2 divisors)\n12. \\(12 = p_1^2 p_2^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\\). (6 divisors)\n13. \\(13 = p_1^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 13\\). (2 divisors)\n14. \\(14 = p_1^1 p_2^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 2, 7, 14\\). (4 divisors)\n15. \\(15 = p_1^1 p_2^1\\) has divisors: \\(1, 3, 5, 15\\). (4 divisors)\n\nFrom the above calculations, we see that the number 12 has the greatest number of divisors, which is 6.\n\nThus, the number with the greatest number of divisors is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
